1. Decoupling the Execution Layer and IP Routing:
When an agent runs locally on an end-user’s bare metal (via local inference engines on sovereign silicon), the physical socket connection, HTTP requests, and IP packet origins transfer entirely to the end-user’s network interface controller (NIC).

2. Transferring CFAA Section 1030 Liability to the Local Operator:
By decentralizing model execution to the local edge, the developer decouples itself from the *actus reus* of network transmission. Local bare-metal execution legally classifies the model weights as a static software tool (analogous to a compiler or standard browser binary), insulating corporate balance sheets from non-contractual Section 1030(g) mass torts.

3. Refuting the Monopoly Immunity Myth & Fifth Amendment Takings Limits:
A core bull-case delusion assumes pure-play labs will achieve an absolute AGI monopoly, forcing courts and Congress to grant them retroactive sovereign immunity. This fails on two grounds:
- **Gross Margin Commoditization:** Multi-vector open-weight competition and aggressive hyperscaler pricing compress API gross margins toward hardware utility rates, while models depreciate within 12–18 months of GPU CapEx outlay—leaving zero excess cash flow to self-insure multi-billion-dollar tort liabilities.
- **Fifth Amendment Takings Clause Barrier:** Under the Fifth Amendment, vested private causes of action for property damage and statutory trespass constitute protected property interests. Congress cannot retroactively extinguish accrued private Section 1030(g) claims to bail out private venture-backed corporations without triggering unconstitutional takings or assuming direct federal Treasury liability.

